
AGENTIC AI Hackathon Rulebook
Tech Zephyr 4.0 | IIT Bhubaneswar

1. Eligibility
Open to AI enthusiasts and students from all institutions and academic backgrounds.
Interdisciplinary and cross-institutional participation is allowed.
Participants must fulfil all registration requirements specified by the organizers.
2. Team Formation
Teams must consist of 1–4 members.
Each participant can be a member of only one team.
Team members must be finalized during registration.
Any change in team composition after the registration deadline requires approval from the Organizing Committee.
A participant cannot contribute to multiple competing teams.
3. Competition Format
The hackathon will be conducted in two stages:
Stage 1 — Online Submission Round
Teams will submit their Agentic AI solution for evaluation.
Stage 2 — Grand Finale
Shortlisted teams will compete in an offline Grand Finale at IIT Bhubaneswar.
A new problem statement will be provided at the beginning of the Grand Finale, requiring teams to design, build, or adapt an Agentic AI solution.
4. Problem Statement & Solution
Participants may choose a meaningful problem domain for Stage 1.
The proposed solution must demonstrate genuine agentic behavior.
A basic chatbot or simple LLM wrapper will not be considered sufficient.
The system should demonstrate capabilities such as planning, decision-making, multi-step execution, tool interaction, evaluation, or adaptation.
Teams must be able to explain why an agentic approach is appropriate for their chosen problem.
5. Technology & AI Tools
Teams are free to choose their technology stack.
LLMs, APIs, open-source models, agent frameworks, RAG, databases, cloud services, and other relevant tools may be used.
Generative AI tools may be used for coding, debugging, research, brainstorming, documentation, prototyping, and testing.
Teams remain fully responsible for their submissions and must understand their implementation.
Judges may ask questions regarding the architecture, code, models, tools, and design decisions.
6. Stage 1 Submission Requirements
Each team must submit:
A presentation providing your approach, solution, challenges and conclusion. 
System Architecture / Workflow (either on GitHub or on the presentation).
Source Code / GitHub Repository.
3–5-minute Demo Video.
Runnable or Deployed Version, wherever applicable.
The submission should clearly demonstrate the working of the proposed system.
Teams must not include API keys, passwords, tokens, or other confidential credentials in their repository.
7. Demo Requirements
The demonstration should showcase an actual workflow of the system, including relevant stages such as:
Goal → Decision → Action → Evaluation → Adaptation → Outcome
Teams are encouraged to demonstrate how their system responds to failures, unexpected inputs, tool failures, or changing conditions.
8. Stage 2 — Grand Finale
Only shortlisted teams will qualify for the Grand Finale.
The Grand Finale will be conducted offline at IIT Bhubaneswar.
A new challenge will be provided at the beginning of the round which will have to be submitted within 24 hours.
Teams will have to give a presentation of their round 1 solution also.
Teams will be evaluated on problem-solving, technical execution, agentic design, adaptability, and decision-making.
Final Round Format 
. Presentation For the Round 1 PS: 
7 Minutes: Presentation + Live Demonstration
5 Minutes: Technical Q&A.
The presentation should cover the problem interpretation, approach, architecture, agentic workflow, live demonstration, key decisions, and results.
. For the Round 2 PS: (Only submission-based evaluation) 
                    [The presentation will be after the 24-hours hackathon.]
. Both the Submission for round 2 & the presentation for the Round 1 will carry 50% weightage each.


9. Live Demonstration Rules
The final demonstration must be conducted live.
Teams must be able to run and explain their system before the judging panel.
Teams must explain the role of major components and the reasoning behind key decisions.
Pre-recorded demonstrations, mocked outputs, or fabricated results cannot replace the required live demonstration.
Teams are responsible for bringing the hardware and required environment necessary to run their solution, subject to organizer instructions.
The submissions for the round 1 and round 2 
10. Originality & Fair Play
The submitted work must be the team's own work.
The following are prohibited:
Plagiarism
Unauthorized copying of code
Submission of another team's work
Sharing solutions between competing teams
Fabrication or manipulation of results
Misrepresentation of third-party work
Unauthorized access to another team's system
Deliberate exploitation of the judging process
Third-party libraries, APIs, models, and datasets may be used where permitted and must be appropriately acknowledged.
11. Responsible AI
Projects must not intentionally facilitate:
Illegal activities
Violence or physical harm
Fraud
Harassment or discrimination
Privacy violations
Unauthorized access
Malicious exploitation
Other harmful or dangerous activities
Teams working with sensitive areas or personal data must clearly state relevant limitations and safeguards.
12. Code of Conduct
All participants are expected to maintain a professional and respectful environment.
Harassment, discrimination, intimidation, abusive behavior, threats, misconduct, or deliberate disruption of the event may result in removal from the event and/or disqualification.
13. Disqualification
A team may be disqualified for:
Violation of eligibility or team rules
Plagiarism or unauthorized copying
Multiple-team participation
Submission of another team's work
Manipulation or fabrication of results
Unauthorized or malicious activity
Misrepresentation of the solution
Violation of the Code of Conduct
Failure to meet submission requirements
Any other serious violation of the event rules

14. Judging & Final Authority
The decision of the judging panel will be final and binding.
Judges may request technical explanations, demonstrations, or supporting information.
The Organizing Committee reserves the right to address unforeseen circumstances not explicitly covered in this rulebook.
The organizers may modify schedules, deadlines, round structure, technical requirements, evaluation procedures, or venue if required.



16. Event Details
Event: Agentic AI Hackathon
Fest: Tech Zephyr 4.0
Institute: Indian Institute of Technology Bhubaneswar
Team Size: 1–4 Members
Format: Online Submission → Offline Grand Finale
Prize Pool: ₹35,000


For registration, timelines, announcements, and further instructions, participants should refer to the official Tech Zephyr 4.0 communication channels.

